# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses f4c9d13865f3, 2026-10-19
thinking All three claims keep one sense in every statement that uses them.

thinking: In P1, the conditional, "thinking" is the act of wishing to think all false. In P2 it is the same act, asserted to be happening. Nothing shifts.

indubitable: P3 asserts the certainty. A1 makes the certainty the condition for accepting the principle. Same claim both times.

accept_first: A1 and C both say it is permitted to accept the cogito as first principle. Same sense.

The one place a reader might hesitate is "think". It could be read narrowly as doubting or broadly as any thought. But each statement uses the same wording, the act of wishing to think all false. So it is one sense.thinking	one
indubitable	one
accept_first	one
